# General Coding Prohibitions

## PROH-01 — Prohibit unnecessary defensive guards

**Do not add defensive checks or fallback values for guaranteed data.** 

Avoid using optional chaining, nullish coalescing (`??`), or early return guards for values that are guaranteed by the type system, data models, or established invariants to always be present. **Unnecessary guards create dead code paths**, mask downstream typing or architectural issues, and violate the principle of simplicity by implying a state that cannot logically exist.

```pseudocode
// bad
function processList(items)
  let safeItems = items ?? []
  for item in safeItems
    print item.name

// good
function processList(items)
  for item in items
    print item.name
```

## PROH-02 — Prohibit overly broad function parameters

**Do not define function parameters with union types or overly permissive shapes when the function only needs a narrow type.** 

Signatures must strictly reflect the exact requirements of the function. Using overly broad parameters (e.g., accepting both an un-normalized and a normalized object) confuses callers, falsely implies the function handles complex cases, and leads to redundant internal type-checking and normalization. **Keep input contracts strict and precise.**

```pseudocode
// bad
function formatData(data: RawData | FormattedData)
  if isFormatted(data)
    return data
  return applyFormatting(data)

// good
function formatData(data: RawData)
  return applyFormatting(data)
```